  /* ===== additions ===== */
  "bar-fugue": {
    what:"A fugue is a contrapuntal form in which one theme (the subject) is imitated in turn by every voice, then developed continuously through entries and connecting episodes.",
    dec:"How can one short idea generate an entire piece purely through imitation?",
    seq:["Exposition","Episode","Middle entries","Episode","Stretto (optional)","Final cadence"],
    sections:[
      {n:"Subject", fn:"States the theme that will generate the whole piece.", listen:"A short, distinctive tune you'll learn to track wherever it returns.", compose:"Write a 4–8 bar line with a clear rhythm and one striking interval — and make sure it works against itself."},
      {n:"Answer", fn:"Re-states the subject in a second voice, transposed (usually to the dominant).", listen:"The same tune entering a fifth higher / fourth lower, overlapping the first voice.", compose:"Choose a 'real' (exact) or 'tonal' (slightly adjusted) answer so the key doesn't run away."},
      {n:"Countersubject", fn:"Recurring counterpoint against later entries (optional).", listen:"A second steadier line that keeps coming back alongside the subject.", compose:"Write counterpoint that fits both subject and answer and inverts cleanly."},
      {n:"Episode", fn:"Modulates between entries; relieves the subject.", listen:"The subject vanishes; little fragments sequence and drift to new keys.", compose:"Take a 1–2 bar motif from the subject and sequence it to pivot to a related key."},
      {n:"Stretto", fn:"Compresses entries for the climax (optional).", listen:"Entries pile up, each starting before the last has finished.", compose:"Overlap subject entries as tightly as the counterpoint allows; save it for late."} ],
    recognize:[
      "The same theme re-enters voice by voice, staggered in time.",
      "Independent, equal melodic lines — not melody plus accompaniment.",
      "Episodes (subject absent) alternate with entries (subject present).",
      "Often a climactic stretto where entries overlap.",
      "Ends with a firm cadence in the home key (often a Picardy third in minor)." ],
    failure:[
      {m:"A subject that is too long or shapeless", why:"the ear can't recognise its returns — and recognising the subject IS the form."},
      {m:"Accompaniment creeps in", why:"voices stop being independent and it collapses into melody-plus-chords; the texture is the form."},
      {m:"Episodes unrelated to the subject", why:"the piece loses unity — episodes must be spun from the subject's own fragments."},
      {m:"Never leaving the home key", why:"every entry sounds identical; with no harmonic journey there is no drama."},
      {m:"Stretto/climax spent too early", why:"the most intense device arrives with nowhere left to go." } ],
    his:"The fugue crystallised in the Baroque (17th–early 18th c.) and J.S. Bach (d.1750) brought it to its summit; it then became the benchmark of compositional craft for centuries.",
    evo:{ from:"the Renaissance imitative motet, the ricercar and the canzona.", became:"the 'fugato' embedded inside Classical/Romantic sonatas & symphonies, and fugal writing in jazz (Mingus) and film." },
    works:[
      {w:"J.S. Bach — The Well-Tempered Clavier (1722, 1742)",why:"Flagship: 48 preludes and fugues in every key — shows the full range of fugal technique."},
      {w:"J.S. Bach — The Art of Fugue (c.1740s)",why:"One subject explored through every device (inversion, augmentation, stretto, mirror)."},
      {w:"J.S. Bach — Fugue in G minor BWV 578 ('Little')",why:"A clear, compact model for first study — easy to hear the entries."},
      {w:"Beethoven — Grosse Fuge, Op.133 (1825)",why:"Demonstrates the fugue pushed to Romantic harmonic extremes."},
      {w:"J.S. Bach — Toccata and Fugue in D minor, BWV 565",why:"Edge case: famous but atypical, and its Bach authorship is disputed — do not learn the norm from it."} ],
    rel:["bar-canon","ren-ricercar","bar-invention","ren-motet"],
    neighbors:[
      {id:"bar-canon", note:"Strictest imitation — the follower copies the leader exactly. A fugue is freer: it develops the subject rather than just duplicating it."},
      {id:"ren-ricercar", note:"The fugue's direct ancestor — same imitative idea but more sectional and archaic."},
      {id:"bar-invention", note:"A two- or three-voice teaching fugue — identical logic at a smaller, learnable scale."},
      {id:"ren-motet", note:"The vocal cousin: imitation driven by text, one 'point' per phrase, instead of one recurring subject."} ],
    concepts:{ rep:"Maximal — the subject recurs constantly across all voices.", con:"Episodes contrast with entries; voices contrast in register and direction.", dir:"The tonal plan and the stretto drive toward the final cadence.", seg:"Entries vs episodes segment an otherwise continuous flow." },
    workflow:[
      "Compose a short, characterful SUBJECT (4–8 bars). Sing it; if you can't remember it, simplify it.",
      "Write the ANSWER: transpose the subject to the dominant; adjust a note or two (tonal answer) if a real answer would derail the key.",
      "Build the EXPOSITION: bring in each voice in turn — subject, answer, subject, answer — until all voices have stated the theme.",
      "Optionally add a COUNTERSUBJECT under the later entries: counterpoint that returns each time and inverts against the subject.",
      "Write an EPISODE: sequence a fragment of the subject through related keys to travel away from home.",
      "Alternate MIDDLE ENTRIES (subject in new keys, different voices) with episodes — vary who carries the subject.",
      "INTENSIFY near the end: try a stretto (overlapping entries) and/or a dominant pedal point.",
      "RESOLVE: a final subject entry in the home key and a firm cadence (Picardy third if you're in minor)." ]
  },
