  "med-organum-par": {
    what:"Organum is the earliest Western polyphony: a plainchant is sung together with an added voice. In its first form (parallel organum) the added voice simply shadows the chant at a fixed interval — one melody becomes two.",
    dec:"How can one melody become two voices?",
    seq:["Chant (vox principalis)","Added voice (vox organalis) in parallel","Oblique motion at cadences"],
    sections:[
      {n:"Vox principalis (chant)", fn:"The original plainchant — the authority the music is built on.", listen:"A familiar chant melody, unchanged.", compose:"Start from an EXISTING chant; do not invent a new tune."},
      {n:"Vox organalis (added voice)", fn:"The new voice that turns one line into two.", listen:"A second voice shadowing the chant at a fixed interval (often a 4th or 5th).", compose:"Add a voice at a perfect interval, moving in parallel with the chant note-for-note."},
      {n:"Cadential adjustment", fn:"Avoids the forbidden tritone and shapes phrase-ends.", listen:"At phrase endings the voices converge or hold instead of strict parallel.", compose:"Near cadences let the organal voice hold or move obliquely to dodge the tritone and close cleanly."} ],
    recognize:[
      "Two voices moving mostly in parallel at a fixed interval (4th, 5th or octave).",
      "Built on a recognisable plainchant.",
      "An open, hollow sonority — perfect intervals, almost no thirds.",
      "Latin sacred text, unaccompanied voices." ],
    failure:[
      {m:"Strict parallel tritones", why:"rigid parallel 4ths/5ths eventually hit the 'diabolus in musica'; the system's main job is to bend around it."},
      {m:"Inventing a free new tune instead of building on chant", why:"that is a LATER development — parallel organum's whole premise is doubling an existing chant."},
      {m:"Basing it on sweet thirds and sixths", why:"that sonority belongs to later (English) practice — wrong sound-world for early organum."} ],
    his:"Organum is first described c.900 in the treatise Musica enchiriadis and developed through the 11th–12th centuries — the foundational moment when Western music became polyphonic.",
    evo:{ from:"monophonic Gregorian chant.", became:"florid melismatic (Notre-Dame) organum → the motet → the whole Western polyphonic tradition." },
    works:[
      {w:"«Rex caeli Domine» (Musica enchiriadis, c.900)",why:"Flagship: a textbook example of strict parallel organum from the very treatise that first describes it."},
      {w:"Winchester Troper (11th c.)",why:"One of the earliest large practical collections of organum."},
      {w:"Léonin — Magnus liber organi (c.1170)",why:"Shows the next stage: the organal voice blossoms into long melismas over held chant notes."},
      {w:"Pérotin — «Viderunt omnes» (c.1200)",why:"Parallel doubling has grown into full four-voice polyphony — organum's destination."} ],
    rel:["med-organum-mel","med-gregorian","med-notredame","ren-motet"],
    neighbors:[
      {id:"med-gregorian", note:"The monophonic source: organum takes a single chant and adds voices to it."},
      {id:"med-organum-mel", note:"The next stage — the added voice breaks free into florid melisma over a sustained chant."},
      {id:"med-notredame", note:"Where organum matured into measured, rhythmic polyphony (Léonin, Pérotin)."},
      {id:"ren-motet", note:"The descendant: independent imitative voices, no longer just doubling a chant."} ],
    concepts:{ rep:"The chant is doubled — 'repeated' simultaneously by the second voice.", con:"Minimal: the voices move together; real contrast arrives only in later polyphony.", dir:"Low — the music follows the chant's own contour.", seg:"Phrase structure follows the chant's text phrases." },
    workflow:[
      "Choose a short PLAINCHANT phrase as your vox principalis — don't compose a new tune.",
      "Pick a parallel INTERVAL: a perfect 5th or 4th below the chant (octave doublings optional).",
      "Write the vox organalis moving in PARALLEL with the chant at that interval, note for note.",
      "Check every vertical interval for TRITONES (e.g. F against B); where one appears, switch to oblique or contrary motion.",
      "At phrase ENDINGS, let the voices converge to a unison or octave for a clean cadence.",
      "To go further, free the organal voice into several notes per chant note — and you have reached melismatic organum." ]
  },
